WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.440
So I'm almost in a sense redoing a story from
January this year,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.160
which is that Carla Harris is again being
accused of flip flopping and backing away

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.440
from Medicare for all.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.910
But this time I actually don't think she is.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.330
I think there is a flip flop here, but it's
a different one from the one that most people

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.330
are talking about.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:17.900
So let me set this up for you.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:23.570
Um, we need to be really clear about the terrible
way in which the healthcare questions were

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:27.890
asked to the Democratic primary candidates
in night one and night two of the debate.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:33.690
The main problem is the way that those questions
were framed was bound to lead to confusion.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.220
And I wish that candidates had actually called
out the framing of those questions, but I'll

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.170
back up and we'll get to that.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:44.290
Back in January, Kamala Harris did a CNN town
hall and during the CNN town call town hall,

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:45.960
she was asked about Medicare for all.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.629
And here's what she had to say.

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:52.760
What is your solution to ensure that people
have access to quality health care at an affordable

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:53.760
price?

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.300
And does that solution involve cutting insurance
companies as we know them out of the equation?

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:03.900
I believe the solution, and I'm, and I'm actually
feel very strongly about this, is that we

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.329
need to have medicare for all.

00:01:06.329 --> 00:01:07.329
That's just the bottom line.

00:01:07.329 --> 00:01:09.200
So that was very definitive.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.299
But then the day after that she backed off
and advisor of hers put out a statement and

00:01:14.299 --> 00:01:18.609
saying, well, she's open to more moderate
ideas as well, like preserving d in the industry

00:01:18.609 --> 00:01:24.529
we have and making tweaks to Obamacare and
it appeared to be a significant backing off

00:01:24.529 --> 00:01:26.240
from Medicare for all.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.539
So then the debate happens late last week
and the day after Carla Harris is interviewed

00:01:31.539 --> 00:01:36.689
and once again the accusation start flying
that Kamala Harris is flip-flopping that during

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:40.200
the debate she raised her hand and said, I'm
for Medicare for all.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.389
And that the day after the debate she backed
off of that and said, I'm not actually for

00:01:44.389 --> 00:01:45.859
Medicare for all.

00:01:45.859 --> 00:01:50.579
There is a flip flop here, but it is not the
one that is being reported at Camila.

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:55.489
Harris w said she was for Medicare for all
in the town hall in January.

00:01:55.489 --> 00:01:58.779
The next day she did sort of back off of it.

00:01:58.779 --> 00:02:02.179
And now she is again saying that she, she's
for Medicare for all.

00:02:02.179 --> 00:02:06.280
Uh, the confusion is again, how that question
was asked.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:12.060
So net let's now look, and this is mostly
visual, this is when the candidates were asked,

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.569
raise your hand on healthcare, but listen
to how the question was framed.

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:21.760
Who here would abolish their private health
insurance in favor of a government plan?

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.560
Yeah.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.370
Right.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.330
So Kamala Harris raises her hand there.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:33.110
And as I said at the time, this was a terrible,
terrible question.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:34.620
This led to confusion.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:40.010
This led to people being able to just go a
cop out claim they didn't understand it, whatever.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:45.159
And case in point, Kamala Harris was interviewed
on CBS the day after the debate and she said,

00:02:45.159 --> 00:02:47.349
Oh yeah, I raised my hand.

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:52.420
But the question was, would I give up my private
insurance for a government plan?

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.880
Not should I, uh, would I want private insurance
abolished?

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.890
And then she gets into an argument with the
interviewer.

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:03.810
The interviewer says, no, no, the question
was should private insurance be made illegal?

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:09.519
And I called this question out because it
was improperly asked for exactly this reason

00:03:09.519 --> 00:03:13.810
and then Camilla Harris gives us, I guess
what you would call her real position on this

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:15.380
issue at this point in time.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:19.459
Senator a another defining moment in the debate
was when every candidate was asked to raise

00:03:19.459 --> 00:03:23.030
their hand if they would abolish private insurance.

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:27.420
In that moment you raised your hand, but afterwards
your campaign says, actually that's not your

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.420
position.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:32.480
So what is your position on private insurance?

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.099
So the question was would you be willing to
give up your private insurance?

00:03:37.099 --> 00:03:40.189
That's not how it was such a plan and that's
what you heard.

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:41.189
Right.

00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:42.189
Okay.

00:03:42.189 --> 00:03:43.790
Then yeah, that's certainly what I heard.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:49.209
And I'm in terms of, I am supportive of Medicare
for all and under Medicare for all policy,

00:03:49.209 --> 00:03:53.709
private insurance would certainly exist and
for supplemental, um, coverage, but under

00:03:53.709 --> 00:03:57.709
Medicare for all and my vision of it, we would
actually extend benefits.

00:03:57.709 --> 00:04:02.620
So, for example, vision care, dental care,
hearing aids, which currently are not covered

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:06.170
in, and anyone you know who is a senior can
tell you they're extremely expensive and people

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:08.170
have to come out of pocket to pay for them.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:13.280
I'm included in my perspective on the issue
of private insurance is this, and I told the

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:19.030
story last night, look, any night in America,
there is some parent who's got a child who

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:25.169
has a fever that's out of control and going
to the emergency room to get help in the middle

00:04:25.169 --> 00:04:26.310
of the night.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:30.600
And then looking at those sliding glass doors
as they're in their car, in the parking lot,

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.110
concerned that if they walk through those
sliding glass doors, they're going to be out

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:36.910
of pocket $5,000 because of the deductible.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:38.740
They've got insurance, all right?

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:42.370
They're going to be out of pocket $5,000 we're
going to have to leave it there.

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:48.400
So you know, 91% and I'll just add 91% of
the doctors in America today are in Medicare,

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.830
so you won't lose your doctor.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:54.889
So I don't actually see this as a flip flop
from the debate in the way that I understood

00:04:54.889 --> 00:05:00.590
the question, but the whole point is the question
was terribly asked what did they actually

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:01.690
mean by the question?

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:06.950
Were they saying would you personally given
the option of giving up your private insurance

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:09.720
for a government plan opt for that plan.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.400
That was a reasonable way to understand the
question.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.350
Others understood it to be this sort of right
wing meme talking point of Democrats would

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:23.220
make private illegal if such a government
plan was put forward and the question by virtue

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:28.130
of being a terrible question and improperly
asked led to exactly this type of thing.

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:31.180
So I don't see the flip flop that some people
are claiming.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:35.639
I don't see that Camilo raising her hand means
yes to Medicare for all.

00:05:35.639 --> 00:05:38.100
And then in the interview she said she's against
it.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:42.729
My interpretation of the Commonwealth flip
flop is that in the town hall she said, I'm

00:05:42.729 --> 00:05:47.520
for Medicare for all and got the political
benefit of that the next day.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:51.410
A statement was put out in January saying,
well, she's open to other options as well,

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:57.110
but she likes Medicare for all and now apparently,
uh, putting her finger to the wind and recognizing

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:01.360
that this is not a good path forward if she
is not for Medicare for all.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:05.009
Look at how Joe Biden is suffering as a result
of his position on healthcare.

00:06:05.009 --> 00:06:08.770
She now again is saying, Oh yeah, I am just
for Medicare for all.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:14.669
So bottom line, it does seem that commonly
Harris is on the Medicare for all train right

00:06:14.669 --> 00:06:15.710
now.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:21.070
But remember she can very easily pay lip service
to it and say, I'm in favor of it without

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:24.630
having any real plan or goal of implementing
it.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:30.780
If she were to become president of the United
States, we need clarity from our elected officials,

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:34.040
from the candidates in particular about what
their policy is.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:38.199
And you have to understand, is someone saying,
I'm for Medicare for all.

00:06:38.199 --> 00:06:43.830
If you don't follow that up and say, okay,
if instead what was proposed were simply some

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:47.129
tweaks to Obamacare, would that be okay with
you?

00:06:47.129 --> 00:06:49.110
Unless you ask that follow up.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:53.670
It's very easy to say, I'm for Medicare for
all and I'm for the public option and I'm

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:58.569
for tweaking Obamacare and I'm for also not
really making too many changes other than

00:06:58.569 --> 00:07:00.710
maybe expanding access to Medicaid.

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:05.860
You've got to go further and this is why the
debate question fell so, so flat where now

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:09.099
we're arguing about which is the flop.

00:07:09.099 --> 00:07:14.930
If there is one that Kamala Harris made, rather
than a better asked question during the debate

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:19.569
would have given us much more valuable information
about what these candidates actually stand

00:07:19.569 --> 00:08:38.029
for.

